<% content_for :title, "Administration guide · SpecGuard" %>

<%# == The public administration guide (SPGD-762)
    ================================================================================================

    WHO THIS PAGE IS FOR. The person who has just minted an `sgu_` key on `/account`, and the agent
    they hand it to. It inherits the integration guide's charter verbatim — ungated, complete, and
    wrong-by-definition wherever it disagrees with the server — and differs from it in SCOPE: that
    page documents the CI-facing `sgk_` surface, this one documents the `sgu_` administration
    surface. See `config/routes.rb`, which draws the same line for the same reason.

    THE FINDING IT EXISTS TO CLOSE, and the reason the grant section is not an appendix.

    `POST /api/v1/repositories` redeems a `GithubRegistrationGrant` — what GitHub said about this
    person, snapshotted while a browser session still held a token that could ask. That snapshot is
    captured in exactly one place (`GithubRepositoryListing#github_sources`, reached only from the
    two controllers that render a repository list in a BROWSER) and it expires after
    `GithubRegistrationGrant::MAX_AGE`. So the headline gesture — mint a key, hand it to an agent —
    fails for anybody who has not opened their repository list in a browser recently, and until this
    page existed that precondition was stated NOWHERE in advance: only afterwards, in the 400, and
    only to the agent rather than to the person who minted the key.

    That is why it is section two, above the exchange it gates, and why the bound is published from
    the constant rather than typed. A page that buried it under the curl snippet would reproduce the
    original defect in a more comfortable location.

    WHAT IT DOES NOT DO. It promises no capability the API does not currently have, and it covers
    exactly the two founding endpoints of the `sgu_` surface: the list and the registration. The
    surface has grown since this page was first drafted — deleting and renaming a repository,
    minting and revoking a repository's CI keys, listing and revoking a repository's agent keys,
    the registrable listing and member management are all served over the API today, and the
    SpecGuard MCP bridge carries tools over these same credentials — and none of that is
    documented here. Nothing on this page describes any of it as absent, and none of its request
    lines appear on this page, because a contract this page has not verified is not this page's
    to publish. Where a reader needs a gesture that only the browser has, the page says so
    plainly and sends them there.
%>

<% endpoint = administration_guide_endpoint %>

<%= render UI::PageComponent.new do %>
  <%= render UI::PageHeaderComponent.new(
        title: "Administration guide",
        subtitle: "Register repositories and manage them with a personal API key — from an agent, " \
                  "or from curl."
      ) %>

  <%= render UI::PanelComponent.new(title: "Start here", id: "start-here") do %>
    <div class="space-y-4">
      <p class="text-sm text-app-content-secondary">
        A <strong class="text-app-content">personal API key</strong> authenticates as
        <em>you</em> — across every repository you can open, rather than as one repository. It is
        what lets an agent register a repository on your behalf and list what you already have.
        It is a different credential from the one CI uses, and the two are not interchangeable.
      </p>

      <%= render UI::DefListComponent.new(rows: [
            ["You need an endpoint", endpoint],
            ["You need a personal key", "Mint one on your account page"],
            ["You need a current grant", "Open your repository list in a browser first"]
          ]) %>

      <%# The third row is the one nothing else on any surface says in advance, so it is stated
          twice: once here as a precondition alongside the other two, and once in full below. A
          reader who skims only this list still learns it exists. %>

      <div class="space-y-2">
        <p class="section-label text-app-content-secondary">The two endpoints this page covers</p>
        <%= render UI::TableComponent.new(columns: ["Endpoint", "What it is for"]) do %>
          <tr>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs break-all text-app-content">GET <%= endpoint %>/api/v1/repositories</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary">
              Everything you can open, owned and shared alike, with each one's delivery health.
              It changes nothing.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs break-all text-app-content">POST <%= endpoint %>/api/v1/repositories</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary">
              Registering one. Returns a <code>201</code> carrying the repository and a freshly
              minted CI key for it.
            </td>
          </tr>
        <% end %>
      </div>

      <%# The credential seam. Stated as a table because the failure it prevents is a 401 that looks
          like an authentication bug and is really a wrong-credential one — and because "which key
          answers which endpoint" is a lookup, not a paragraph.

          Deliberately NOT titled as a count. "The two credentials" was true when this table was
          written and stopped being true the day agent keys landed on the account page — the same
          defect the integration guide's charter comment once carried about its route count. A
          table that says WHICH KEY answers WHAT survives the fourth credential; one that says HOW
          MANY there are has to be re-counted, and will not be.

          The `Answers` cells name the endpoints THIS PAGE covers, not every endpoint each
          credential answers — the `sgu_` surface grew long before this table was drawn. The row
          for the agent key is here because one of this page's two endpoints answers it too: an
          `sga_` key can read the list, and cannot register. %>
      <div class="space-y-2">
        <p class="section-label text-app-content-secondary">Which credential answers what</p>
        <%= render UI::TableComponent.new(columns: ["Key", "Speaks for", "Answers", "Minted on"]) do %>
          <tr>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-app-content">sgu_…</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary">You, the person</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-app-content-secondary break-all">
              GET and POST /api/v1/repositories
            </td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary">
              <%= link_to "Your account page", account_path, class: "text-app-cta hover:underline" %>
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-app-content">sga_…</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary">Nobody — only what the key was granted</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-app-content-secondary break-all">
              GET /api/v1/repositories — reads only; registering refuses it
            </td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary">
              <%= link_to "Your account page", account_path, class: "text-app-cta hover:underline" %>
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-app-content">sgk_…</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary">One repository</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-app-content-secondary break-all">
              /api/v1/repository, /api/v1/ingest
            </td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary">That repository's page</td>
          </tr>
        <% end %>
        <p class="text-xs text-app-muted">
          They refuse each other. A <code>sgu_</code> key sent to an endpoint expecting
          <code>sgk_</code> gets a <code>401</code>, and the reverse is also a <code>401</code> —
          not a permissions error, and not a sign the key is broken. The near-identical paths
          <code>/repository</code> and <code>/repositories</code> are separate surfaces for exactly
          this reason, so neither can quietly serve the wrong thing. An <code>sga_</code> key is
          different again: it is authenticated on the endpoints it may read, and a person-only
          action such as registering answers it with a <code>403</code> rather than a 401 — the
          key is known, and is simply not allowed to act as you.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  <% end %>

  <%= render UI::PanelComponent.new(title: "Your personal key", id: "personal-key") do %>
    <div class="space-y-4">
      <p class="text-sm text-app-content-secondary">
        Mint one on
        <%= link_to "your account page", account_path, class: "text-app-cta hover:underline" %>.
        Hold as many as you need — one per agent, per laptop, per anything — and revoke them one at
        a time; revoking one leaves the rest working.
      </p>

      <%= render UI::AlertComponent.new(tone: :warning, title: "Shown once, and replaced rather than rotated") do %>
        <p class="mt-1">
          The token is displayed at the moment you mint it and never again — SpecGuard stores only a
          SHA-256 digest, so it cannot show you an existing key even if you ask. Store it where the
          agent that needs it will read it from.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2">
          There is also <strong>no rotation</strong> for this credential, unlike a repository's CI
          key. Replacing one is two gestures in a browser: mint the new key, then revoke the old.
          Doing it in that order means nothing is ever without a working credential.
        </p>
      <% end %>
    </div>
  <% end %>

  <%# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      THE SECTION THIS PAGE WAS WRITTEN FOR. Above the exchange it gates, deliberately.
  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- %>
  <%= render UI::PanelComponent.new(title: "Before you register: the browser step", id: "grant") do %>
    <div class="space-y-4">
      <%= render UI::AlertComponent.new(
            tone: :warning,
            title: "Registering over the API needs something a key alone cannot give it"
          ) do %>
        <p class="mt-1">
          Open your repository list in a browser at least once every
          <strong><%= administration_guide_grant_max_age %></strong>. Without that, registration is
          refused however valid your key is.
        </p>
      <% end %>

      <p class="text-sm text-app-content-secondary">
        SpecGuard will not register a repository without checking with GitHub that you administer
        it. Asking GitHub that question needs a credential that speaks for <em>you</em> on GitHub —
        and a request carrying only an API key does not have one, by design: that credential lives
        in a browser session and is deliberately stored nowhere else.
      </p>

      <p class="text-sm text-app-content-secondary">
        So the check is not skipped, and it is not asked live either. When you view your repository
        list in a browser, SpecGuard records <strong class="text-app-content">what GitHub said about
        you at that moment</strong> — which repositories you can see, and which of those you
        administer. Registering over the API redeems that recording. It costs no extra GitHub
        request and happens whether or not you were thinking about it.
      </p>

      <%= render UI::DefListComponent.new(rows: [
            ["What captures it", "Viewing your repository list in a browser"],
            ["How long it lasts", administration_guide_grant_max_age],
            ["What happens past that", "Registration is refused until you look again"]
          ]) %>

      <p class="text-sm text-app-content-secondary">
        The bound exists because a recording can go stale in a way a live check cannot: somebody who
        has lost admin on a repository would otherwise keep a recording that says otherwise. After
        <%= administration_guide_grant_max_age %> it grants nothing. Signing in and opening the list
        again replaces it, and an account somebody uses regularly never meets the expiry at all.
      </p>

      <%= render UI::AlertComponent.new(tone: :neutral, title: "The refusal, so you can recognise it") do %>
        <p class="mt-1">
          A registration attempted without a current recording comes back as a <code>400</code>
          saying the repository
          <em><%= InstallationRepositories::MESSAGES.fetch(:not_granted) %></em>
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2">
          That is this precondition and nothing else — the key is fine, the repository name is fine.
          Open your repository list in a browser and post the same request again. It is deliberately
          a different sentence from the refusals that mean GitHub answered and the answer was no.
        </p>
      <% end %>
    </div>
  <% end %>

  <%= render UI::PanelComponent.new(title: "Registering a repository", id: "register") do %>
    <div class="space-y-4">
      <p class="text-sm text-app-content-secondary">
        One request registers the repository <strong class="text-app-content">and mints its first CI
        key</strong>, because a repository with no key is one nothing can deliver to. Both come back
        in the same response.
      </p>

      <div class="space-y-2">
        <p class="section-label text-app-content-secondary">The request</p>
        <%= render UI::TableComponent.new(columns: ["Field", "Type", "Required", "What it is"]) do %>
          <tr>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-app-content">github_full_name</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary">string</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary">yes</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary">
              <code>org/repo</code>, exactly as GitHub spells it. Matched case-insensitively.
              Anything that is not two segments is refused.
            </td>
          </tr>
        <% end %>
      </div>

      <%# The fixture. `id` on the WRAPPER rather than on the <code> element, for the reason its
          sibling on the integration guide gives: the Copy button is a sibling inside it, and the
          verification spec wants the block rather than the button.

          The id wrapper holds the fixture ALONE — the label and the caption are siblings outside
          it, not children. That is deliberate and it is a correction of the sibling's shape rather
          than a copy of it: the spec finds the payload as `#<id> code`, and prose sitting inside
          the wrapper is free to contain a <code> tag of its own, which turns that find into an
          ambiguous match. The caption below does exactly that. Keeping the wrapper down to the
          copyable block means the fixture stays unambiguous however the surrounding prose is
          edited. %>
      <div class="space-y-2">
        <p class="section-label text-app-content-secondary">A complete, accepted request body</p>
        <div data-controller="copy-text" id="<%= AdministrationGuideHelper::EXAMPLE_REQUEST_ELEMENT_ID %>">
          <%= render UI::CopyableCodeComponent.new(multiline: true) do %><%= administration_guide_example_request %><% end %>
        </div>
        <p class="text-xs text-app-muted">
          Top-level, not nested under a <code>repository</code> key — this is a JSON API driven by
          an agent, not a Rails form. This exact document is posted to the live endpoint by
          SpecGuard's own test suite, so it is the contract rather than an illustration of it.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-2" data-controller="copy-text">
        <p class="section-label text-app-content-secondary">Posting it</p>
        <%= render UI::CopyableCodeComponent.new(multiline: true) do %><%= administration_guide_register_curl_snippet(endpoint) %><% end %>
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-2">
        <p class="section-label text-app-content-secondary">What comes back</p>
        <%= render UI::TableComponent.new(columns: ["Status", "Meaning"]) do %>
          <tr>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-app-content">201</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary">
              Registered. The body carries a <code>repository</code> block and an
              <code>api_key</code> block.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-app-content">400</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary">
              Refused, with the reason in <code>message</code>. One response for every cause: no
              current recording of your GitHub permissions, GitHub does not list you as an
              administrator, the App is not installed on it, the name is not
              <code>org/repo</code>, or it is already registered. Read the sentence — each names its
              own fix.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-app-content">401</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary">
              The credential is missing, revoked, or is a <code>sgk_</code> repository key rather
              than a <code>sgu_</code> personal one.
            </td>
          </tr>
        <% end %>
      </div>

      <%# The id wraps the copyable block ALONE, for the reason given at the request fixture above:
          a wrapper that also held prose could contain a <code> tag of its own and turn the spec's
          `#<id> code` find into an ambiguous match. This caption happens to carry none today, but
          a caption is free to carry one — the request fixture's above does — and the wrapper is
          kept narrow so that staying unambiguous does not depend on how this prose is edited. %>
      <div class="space-y-2">
        <p class="section-label text-app-content-secondary">The 201 body</p>
        <div data-controller="copy-text" id="<%= AdministrationGuideHelper::EXAMPLE_RESPONSE_ELEMENT_ID %>">
          <%= render UI::CopyableCodeComponent.new(multiline: true) do %><%= administration_guide_example_response %><% end %>
        </div>
        <p class="text-xs text-app-muted">
          The values are illustrative — an id, a timestamp and a token do not exist before the
          request is made. The field names are the contract.
        </p>
      </div>

      <%= render UI::AlertComponent.new(tone: :warning, title: "api_key.token is the only copy that will ever exist") do %>
        <p class="mt-1">
          That value appears in this response and nowhere else, ever. Nothing stores it — SpecGuard
          keeps a digest — and no endpoint can re-serve it. An agent that registers a repository and
          discards the response has minted a credential it cannot recover and cannot look up. The
          way back is to replace it: mint a new key for the repository and revoke the orphan — two
          calls over the API, or the same two gestures in a browser.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2">
          Write it straight into wherever CI reads its secrets, in the same run that made the
          request. The <code>hint</code> beside it is a display fragment for recognising the key
          later in a list — it is not the credential and cannot be used as one.
        </p>
      <% end %>

      <p class="text-sm text-app-content-secondary">
        That token is a repository CI key, and wiring it up is the other guide's subject: see the
        <%= link_to "integration guide", integration_guide_path, class: "text-app-cta hover:underline" %>
        for the reporter, the environment variables and the wire contract for posting runs.
      </p>
    </div>
  <% end %>

  <%= render UI::PanelComponent.new(title: "Listing what you can open", id: "list") do %>
    <div class="space-y-4">
      <p class="text-sm text-app-content-secondary">
        Everything you own, plus everything shared with you through a membership. A repository you
        neither own nor are a member of never enters this response.
      </p>

      <div class="space-y-2" data-controller="copy-text">
        <%= render UI::CopyableCodeComponent.new(multiline: true) do %><%= administration_guide_list_curl_snippet(endpoint) %><% end %>
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-2">
        <p class="section-label text-app-content-secondary">What each entry carries</p>
        <%= render UI::TableComponent.new(columns: ["Field", "What it is"]) do %>
          <tr>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-app-content">id, full_name, name, registered_at</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary">
              The same four fields, under the same names, that every other repository block on the
              API serves.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-app-content">role</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary">
              <code>owner</code> or <code>member</code>. The list mixes both and nothing else
              distinguishes them. Under an agent key — which can read this list too — the value is
              <code>agent</code> for every entry, because the question of whose rights each row
              rides on does not apply to a credential that speaks for nobody.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-app-content">delivery_health</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary">
              <code>refusing</code> and <code>last_rejection_at</code> — whether this repository's
              CI deliveries are currently being thrown away. Served on every entry, refused and
              clean alike: <code>refusing: false</code> is a finding, not an absence.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-app-content">latest_run</td>
            <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary">
              <code>null</code> when CI has never reported; otherwise the newest run CI posted,
              at list depth: <code>ingested_at</code>, <code>branch</code>,
              <code>commit_sha</code>, <code>total_specs</code>,
              <code>annotated_specs</code>, <code>annotated_ratio</code> (a 0–1 fraction, never a
              percentage), <code>suite_size_measured</code>, <code>duration_seconds</code>, and
              <code>shards</code>. A null and a zeroed block are different facts — never-reported
              is not the same thing as ran-and-found-nothing.
            </td>
          </tr>
        <% end %>
      </div>

      <p class="text-sm text-app-content-secondary">
        Ordered by <code>full_name</code>, which is stable enough to diff two readings against each
        other. Nothing here promises an ordering by id.
      </p>
    </div>
  <% end %>

  <%# "What this key cannot do yet" is what this panel used to say, and its list went false three
      times over: the API grew delete, rename, CI-key minting, the registrable listing and member
      management, and — since this branch last read main — the listing and revoking of a
      repository's agent keys (SPGD-1004), with the MCP bridge carrying all of it to agents as
      tools. A page whose charter is "wrong-by-definition wherever it disagrees with the server"
      cannot keep a list of absences that no longer exist. The honest residual is small: personal
      keys, which cannot be minted or revoked over the API; agent-key MINTING, which has no route
      because an agent key's grant is chosen once, at /account, from the minter's own rights; and
      in-place rotation, which the API surface rules out deliberately rather than has not got to
      yet. %>
  <%= render UI::PanelComponent.new(title: "What only the browser can do", id: "limits") do %>
    <div class="space-y-4">
      <p class="text-sm text-app-content-secondary">
        Stated so you do not go looking. This API surface has grown since this guide was first
        drafted — deleting a repository, renaming one, minting and revoking a repository's CI
        keys, listing and revoking a repository's agent keys, listing what may be registered, and
        member management are all served over the API today, and the SpecGuard MCP bridge carries
        them to agents as tools. What is still browser-only, today:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-1 pl-5 text-sm text-app-content-secondary">
        <li>Minting or revoking personal <code>sgu_</code> keys.</li>
        <li>Minting agent <code>sga_</code> keys — revoking one of a repository's agent keys is
            over the API now; the mint is not, because the key's grant is chosen once, at
            mint, on your account page.</li>
        <li>Rotating a repository's CI key <em>in place</em> — the API deliberately offers no
            rotate, because a regenerated token stops the old one working with no grace window.
            Replacing a key over the API is two calls rather than one: mint the new key, then
            revoke the old.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="text-sm text-app-content-secondary">
        Everything on this page is something the API does now. Where you need one of the above,
        <%= link_to "your account page", account_path, class: "text-app-cta hover:underline" %>
        and the repository's own page are where they live.
      </p>
    </div>
  <% end %>
<% end %>
